A Hydrolytically Stable V(IV)-Metal-Organic Framework with Photocatalytic Bacteriostatic Activity for Autonomous Indoor Humidity Control
Water adsorbing materials are of fundamental importance in many fields such as air dehumidification, atmospheric water harvesting, adsorption heat and cooling pumps, and indoor humidity control[1]. Particularly, indoor relative humidity plays a significant role in human daily life which is related to human health[2]. According to the American Society of Heating, Refrigerating, and Air-Conditioning Engineers (ASHRAE), the recommended indoor relative humidity lies in the range of \(45\%\) - \(65\%\), which maintains a healthy and comfortable indoor environment[3]. Many materials, like silica gels and zeolites, have been widely used in adsorbing water and controlling indoor relative humidity[4]. However, these adsorbents suffer from low energy-efficiency during adsorption/desorption cycles because of the high affinity to water molecules[1e]. Moreover, the building walls are made of cement, metal, wood and/or fabric often face the complicated issues of breeding mold, fungi and other bacteria especially under high humidity conditions. Therefore, novel materials featuring both moisture tunability and bacteriostatic activity can be favorable for humidity control in many confined spaces.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), a unique family of highly crystalline and porous materials that are constructed by periodically joining metal ions with organic linkers have attracted
extensive interests by virtue of their structural tunability and diversity[5]. MOFs with their prominent high porosity have shown great potentials in gels separation, storage, catalysis and sensing[6]. Indeed, MOFs with long-term stability and high water uptake hold great promise for water harvesting, adsorption heat pumps (AHP), and desiccant cooling systems (DCS)[7]. Pioneering works using Y-shp-MOF-5[8], Cr-soc-MOF-1[9] and NU-1500[10] for indoor humidity control have shown potentials in this field.
Recently, our group reported the photocatalytic bactericidal properties of a series of MOFs which show potentials in air pollution control and personal protection[11]. Stable MOFs with water uptake capability and antibacterial properties can provide an energy-efficient and cost-effective approach to deal with such problems. Herein, we report a new hydrolytically stable mesoporous MOF, BIT-66, with indoor humidity control and photocatalytic bacteriostatic effect which can be promising candidates for humidity control in many confined spaces (Scheme 1).
Hydrothermal reaction of \(\mathsf{V C l}_{3}\) and 1,3,5-tris(4-carboxyphenyl)benzene (H2BTB) in ultrapure water at \(220~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for three days gave the crystalline powder of BIT-66. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) analysis revealed that BIT-66 is an isoreticular network structure of InPF-110 (Figure 1a and S1)[12]. Notably, compared with InPF-110, the BIT-66 was synthesized in ultrapure water rather than in dimethylformamide (DMF). It is obvious that there are no formate ligands in the structure of BIT-66. In order to unambiguously confirm the structure of BIT-66, the oxidation state of the vanadium sites was further verified by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) (Figure 1b) and electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) (Figure S3) measurements which demonstrated a tetravalent state. Moreover, the elemental analysis and inductively coupled plasma mass spectroscopy
