(ICP-MS) measurements for the BIT-66 indicated the chemical formula was \(\mathsf{V}_{3}(\mathsf{O})_{3}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})(\mathsf{B T B})_{2}\) (Table S1). The thermal stability was evaluated by the thermogravimetric analysis (TGA), showing that the BIT-66 did not decompose up to \(400\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) atmosphere; PXRD and Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) further confirmed the crystallinity and composition were maintained (Figure S4).
According to the reported structure of \(\mathsf{V}^{\mathsf{I V}}_{3}(\mathsf{O})_{3}(\mathsf{T H F})(\mathsf{C}_{6}\mathsf{H}_{5}\mathsf{C O}_{2})_{6}^{[13]}\), we proposed that the BIT-66 should have a similar trinuclear \(\mathsf{6\sigma}\) -centered S60 where the THF ligand is replaced by \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) (Figure S5). Here we combined density functional theory (DFT) and PXRD techniques to optimize and analyze the structure of BIT-66. As a starting geometry, BIT-66 with \(\mathsf{V}_{3}(\mathsf{O})_{3}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})(\mathsf{B T B})_{2}\) motif was constructed from the structure of InPF-110, in which the \(\ln^{3^{+}}\) was replaced by the \(\mathsf{V}^{4^{+}}\), the two terminal formate ligands were changed as \(\mathsf{V}{=}\mathsf{O}\) and the ethanol was substituted by \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\). The structure was then optimized via Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof (PBE) function that implied in Vienna ab initio Simulation Package (VASP), and the computational details are supplied in supporting information. The results revealed that BIT-66 crystallized in the hexagonal system, space group \(\bar{P}\bar{6}2\mathsf{c}\), and there is no obvious difference of lattice parameters between BIT-66 and InPF-110. As shown in Figure 1a, each \(\{\mathsf{V}_{3}(\mathsf{O})_{3}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})(\mathsf{C O O})_{6}\}\) trinuclear SBU is connected with six \(\mathsf{B T B}^{3-}\) ligands to form a 3D framework. The network of BIT-66 exhibits the same binodal topology of InPF-110, denoted 3,6T60, which possess hexagonal channels along the c axis, and the maximum dimension of the channels is around \(2.53\;\mathsf{n m}\) (Figure 1c). Pawley refinement of BIT-66 was carried out with the Material Studio, showing the simulated PXRD pattern matched with the experimental one with low Rwp \((7.95\%)\) and \(R p\) \((5.55\%)\) values (Figure 1d).
The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area and total pore volume were calculated to be \(1417\;\mathsf{m}^{2}\;\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) and \(0.87\;\mathsf{c m}^{3}\;\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) by \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) sorption measurement at \(77\,\,\mathsf{K}\) (Figure 2a). The pore size distribution based on nonlocal DFT method revealed two types of pores with diameters of \(0.65\,\mathsf{n m}\) and \(2.58\,\mathsf{n m}\), respectively (Figure S6). The water stability of BIT-66 was tested by immersing the
activated sample in water at room temperature for \(120\,\mathrm{~h~}\). As shown in Figure 2b and Figure S7, the PXRD patterns and \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) sorption isotherm showed that the BIT-66 maintains its crystallinity and porosity.
Water vapor isotherm of BIT-66 at 298 K was shown in Figure 2c. It revealed an initial water uptake at the first step (below \(55\%\) RH) which could mainly attribute to the open metal sites and micropores adsorption of BIT-66. At higher moisture level (between \(55\%{-}65\%\) RH), a steep uptake was observed and the uptake capacity reached \(55\,\mathsf{w t}\%\) . Maximum water uptake reached \(71\;\mathsf{w t}\%\) at \(98\%\) RH and the overall water vapor sorption isotherm profile showed S-shaped branches with a moderate hysteresis loop at \(45\%{-}60\%\) RH. Markedly, the high water uptake and S-shaped adsorption isotherm are consistent with the suggested working range \((45\%{-}65\%\) RH). Furthermore, we performed water sorption test for 20 cycles at 298 K and non-equilibrium conditions with the adsorption at \(80\%\) RH (25 mbar) and the desorption at \(15\%\) RH (4.8 mbar) (Figure S8). The BIT-66 exhibited remarkable water uptake properties and maintained a working capacity in the range of \(42.9–48.5\;\mathsf{w t}\%\) , indicating that the material exhibits cyclic sorption performance for long-term application. Subsequent measurement of water sorption for 50 cycles is shown in Figure S9. There was a tiny decrease of the working capacity upon 50 cycling process. The crystallinity and porosity of BIT-66 after 50 water adsorption-desorption cycles were confirmed by PXRD (Figure 2b), \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) sorption at \(77\,\mathrm{~K~}\) and pore size distribution calculation (Figure S10), which all indicate BIT-66 is indeed stable without any noticeable changes after water sorption cycles. More importantly, we observed that after 20 cycles the dropping trend of capacity has slowed down to gradually reach a useful plateau over longer cycles (Figure S9, right). Reasonable explanation for this behavior was the incomplete desorption of water at \(15\%\) RH after the adsorption of water at \(80\%\) RH, since the desorption curve cannot completely coincident with the adsorption one in the range of \(5\%{-}40\%\) RH, which might be caused by the following reasons. 1) The pore size distribution based on nonlocal DFT method revealed two types of pores with diameters of \(0.65\ \mathsf{n m}\) and \(2.58\;\mathsf{n m}\), respectively. The water adsorbed in the micropores may need harsh conditions to remove; therefore, the incomplete removal of water in the micropores is likely to affect the sorption behavior in the following cycles. As the water molecules are saturating in the micropores, the working capacity gradually stabilizes. 2) There are also coordinated water molecules on the trimer SBU of BIT-66 (Figure 1a), which can only be removed by
