full activation process. During the desorption process at \(298~\mathsf{K}\) and \(15\%\) RH, these coordinated water molecules will remain at the sites and can also influence the water sorption behavior by hydrogen bonding interactions.
In addition, we selected a wide range of adsorbents from the literatures and compared their water working capacities with BIT\(66^{[7b,8-10]}\). Table S2 shows the dehumidification trigger point and working capacity at \(298~\mathsf{K}\) in the range between \(40\%-65\%\) RH. Only NU-1500-Cr has a higher working capacity than BIT-66, while BIT-66 has a more suitable trigger point.
Considering high moisture environment can induce the growth of mold and/or bacteria, moisture control materials with bacteriostatic effect can be promising candidates for practical applications. We further examined the visible light photocatalytic bacteriostatic performance of BIT-66. To simulate the real application scenario, fully activated BIT-66 sample was mixed with a commercial binder ethylene-vinyl acetate copolymer (EVA) and coated on a steel plate, with a mass of \(0.0100\,\mathfrak{g}\) (90 wt% MOF and \(10\,\,\mathsf{w t}\%\) EVA). The PXRD pattern of BIT-66 coating layer matched well with the powder sample, indicating the retaining of the structure (Figure S11).
The device model for bacteriostatic test is shown in Figure 3a. In this experiment, we chose Escherichia coli (E. coli) (\(10^{6}\) CFU \(\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}\)) as a representative bacterium to evaluate the photocatalytic bacteriostatic performance of BIT-66 coating layers. As shown in Figure 3b, E. coli removal efficiency of BIT-66 coating layer in dark was \(44\%\), and the value reached \(96\%\) after 1-hour irradiation under visible light, while the blank sample under irradiation only gave a removal efficiency of \(47\%\). Meanwhile, the time-dependent changes in the quantity of bacterial growth for blank sample and BIT-66 coating layer both in dark and under visible light were monitored by measuring OD600 (Figure 3c). There was almost no bacteria recovery observed in the following \(48\,\mathfrak{h}\) after removing light, indicating that the E. coli was inhibited by BIT-66 for a long time. Commercial molecular sieve (MCM-41) with moisture controlling character\(^{[9]}\) was also tested for photocatalytic bacteriostasis. It displayed low bacteriostatic activity no matter under light or in dark over \(1\,\mathfrak{h}\) (55% and 52% removal efficiency, respectively) (Figure 3b). The fluorescence staining assays were performed on E. coli that was treated by MCM-41 and BIT-66 in dark and under light. In Figure S12a-h, a large number of residual bacteria were detected on plate coated with MCM-41, whereas very few living bacteria were found on irradiated BIT-66 coating layer, illustrating a much higher antibacterial activity of BIT-66 compared with MCM-41. Although trace amount of leached \(\mathsf{V}^{4+}\) (1.5 ppm) was detected in the eluent after photocatalytic reaction (Table S3), it was shown that \(\mathsf{V}^{4+}\) solution (2 ppm), \(\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{B}\) solution (11.4 ppm) and the mixture of them showed \(<50\%\) bacteriostatic efficiency after \(1\,\mathfrak{h}\) both in dark and under visible light, presenting much lower activity than the BIT-66 coating layer under irradiation (Figure 3d). The result demonstrated that the remarkable photocatalytic bacteriostatic activity came from the intact MOF framework in the presence of visible light. BIT-66 remained crystallinity after photocatalytic reaction under \(1\,\mathfrak{h}\) of light illumination (Figure S13). In a practical scenario, the indoor humidity control adsorbents work in the presence of water vapor, and BIT-66 exhibits great stability under humid environment and the releasing of vanadium ions is almost negligible in this case.
The excellent bacteriostatic activity prompts us to further probe the photocatalytic mechanism. The plot of \((\mathsf{A}h\mathsf{v})^{1/2}\) versus \(h\,\nu\) was shown in Figure S14 which indicated a bandgap of 2.16 eV for BIT-66. The valence band (VB) position of BIT-66 was verified as \(1.71\,\mathrm{eV}\) by the valence bond X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (VB-XPS) (Figure S15). The band position of BIT-66 and the reactive oxygen species (ROS) reaction potential were illustrated in Figure 3e, which indicated that BIT-66 was suitable for superoxide \((\bullet\mathsf{O}_{2}^{-})\) generation for photocatalytic bacteriostasis. The EPR spin-trapping method confirmed the generation of \(\bullet\mathsf{O}_{2}^{-}\) during photocatalysis. No signal was detected in dark whereas six characteristics peaks of DMPO- \(\bullet\mathsf{O}_{2}^{-}\) were observed under visible light, demonstrating the generation of \(\bullet\mathsf{O}_{2}^{-}\) (Figure 3f). In addition, no signal of DMPO- \(\bullet\mathsf{O}_{2}^{-}\) confirmed the absence of \(\bullet\mathsf{O}\mathsf{H}\) for both irradiated and unirradiated BIT-66 samples (Figure S16). The reduced bacteriostatic performance of BIT-66 coating layer in the presence of \(\bullet\mathsf{O}_{2}^{-}\) quencher (SOD) further confirmed the bacteriostatic effect stems from \(\bullet\mathsf{O}_{2}^{-}\) (Figure S17).
In summary, a highly crystalline, mesoporous and water stable V(IV)-MOF, BIT-66, has been synthesized, presenting great humidity control capability and photocatalytic bacteriostatic effect. BIT-66 shows high water uptake and reversible water
## COMMUNICATION
sorption behavior in the recommended indoor humidity range of \(45\%-60\%\) RH. It also presents a unique photocatalytic bacteriostatic performance under visible light, which is beneficial for applying MOFs as the moisture adjusting adsorbents under humid environments especially in confined spaces. These findings may shed light on the design of advanced indoor humid control system in air-conditioned buildings, space shuttles and submarines.
